# THE SECOND EXPERIMENT CLIENT
# ----------------------------
# patches/android/no-nimbus.patch (LW-M4-03) severs the Kotlin/Rust Nimbus SDK,
# the one Fenix drives. It is not the only Nimbus in the build. There is a
# second, completely independent implementation in Gecko itself -- the JS one,
# toolkit/components/nimbus/{ExperimentAPI,lib/*}.sys.mjs -- and
# toolkit/components/moz.build:147 is an unconditional `DIRS += ["nimbus"]`,
# so it ships on Android. docs/android/POLICIES.md section 6 flagged it and
# LW-M1-01 deliberately kept lockPref("app.shield.optoutstudies.enabled", false)
# in the COMMON half of librewolf.cfg (common.cfg:633) precisely because this
# client reads it.
#
# Confirmed from the BUILT APK, not from the source tree
# (~/lw-m5-02/out-iso/apk/fenix-x86_64-debug.apk, versionName 1.0.2634):
#
#   * assets/omni.ja contains all 12 JS modules --
#     chrome/toolkit/res/nimbus/ExperimentAPI.sys.mjs (33,574 bytes),
#     FeatureManifest.sys.mjs (210,912), FirefoxLabs.sys.mjs (3,810) and the
#     nine under lib/, among them RemoteSettingsExperimentLoader.sys.mjs
#     (43,424) and ExperimentManager.sys.mjs (66,599) -- plus the three JSON
#     schemas, i.e. 15 files under chrome/toolkit/res/nimbus/. (An earlier
#     draft of this header said "13 JS modules"; the tree has 12. Counted with
#     `find chrome/toolkit/res/nimbus -type f` on the unpacked omni.ja.)
#   * omni.ja's chrome/chrome.manifest maps `resource nimbus toolkit/res/nimbus/`,
#     so `resource://nimbus/...` really resolves in the shipped APK.
#
# So the component is present and importable. The question this patch had to
# answer is whether it can FETCH or SYNC, and the answer -- before this patch --
# is no, but only by accident, in four separate places. That is why the patch
# exists: four accidents are not a barrier, and every one of them is upstream's
# to change.
#
#
# WHY IT CANNOT FETCH TODAY (four accidents, none of them a decision)
# ------------------------------------------------------------------
# All network traffic in this component goes through exactly one funnel:
# RemoteSettingsExperimentLoader.updateRecipes() -> getRecipesFromAllCollections()
# -> client.sync()/get() on the `nimbus-desktop-experiments` and
# `nimbus-secure-experiments` remote-settings collections. updateRecipes()
# returns immediately unless `this._enabled` is true, which only enable() sets.
#
#  1. NOBODY CALLS init(). ExperimentAPI.init() is the only caller of
#     _rsLoader.enable() (ExperimentAPI.sys.mjs:330). `grep -rn
#     "ExperimentAPI.init"` over the whole tree, tests excluded, returns FOUR
#     call sites -- two under browser/ (never built for Android:
#     browser/components/aboutwelcome/actors/AboutWelcomeParent.sys.mjs:117 and
#     browser/components/defaultwindowslaunchonlogin/
#     DefaultWindowsLaunchOnLogin.sys.mjs:116),
#     toolkit/components/normandy/Normandy.sys.mjs:109 (normandy is added only
#     for MOZ_BUILD_APP == "browser", toolkit/components/moz.build:141 -- and
#     `resource normandy` is absent from the APK's chrome.manifest, so it is
#     genuinely not there), and
#     toolkit/components/backgroundtasks/BackgroundTasksUtils.sys.mjs:300,
#     inside `enableNimbus()` (declared at :299).
#
#     That last one IS packaged on Android: modules/BackgroundTasksUtils.sys.mjs
#     and modules/backgroundtasks/BackgroundTask_message.sys.mjs are both in the
#     APK's omni.ja (--disable-backgroundtasks is not passed, and
#     toolkit/moz.configure:3170 defaults it on). enableNimbus() has two callers
#     in the tree -- BackgroundTask_message.sys.mjs:323 and
#     toolkit/mozapps/update/BackgroundTask_backgroundupdate.sys.mjs:250, the
#     second of which is NOT packaged on Android (no MOZ_UPDATER) -- and both
#     are background tasks, which need `--backgroundtask <name>` on the command line
#     (toolkit/xre/nsAppRunner.cpp:4337). Android has no command line in normal
#     operation -- but a DEBUGGABLE build does: GeckoRuntime reads
#     /data/local/tmp/<pkg>-geckoview-config.yaml and DebugConfig turns its
#     `args:` list into Gecko's argv. That is the same channel
#     scripts/android-smoke.sh uses to pass -remote-allow-system-access. So on
#     the debug APK this is reachable with adb; on a release-configured build it
#     is not.
#
#  2. THE POLL FALLBACK CANNOT ADOPT THE COLLECTION EITHER, and this one is
#     worth spelling out because it is the non-obvious half. RemoteSettings'
#     periodic pollChanges() does not only sync clients somebody instantiated:
#     services/settings/remote-settings.sys.mjs:187-216 `_client()` will build a
#     DEFAULT client for an unregistered collection in the `main` bucket, if
#     `hasLocalData()` or `hasLocalDump()` is true. `hasLocalDump` is false --
#     services/settings/dumps/main/moz.build ships no nimbus dump at all (and
#     explicitly withholds most dumps from `mobile/*`); verified in the built
#     APK, whose whole omni.ja defaults/settings/main/ is password-recipes.json,
#     doh-providers.json and doh-config.json. So the loader's two lazy
#     RemoteSettings clients are never constructed by anyone.
#
#     BUT `hasLocalData` IS TRUE, AND THE RECIPES ARE ON THE DEVICE ANYWAY.
#     This was found by measurement and it contradicts the obvious reading of
#     the paragraph above, so it is stated here rather than in a report.
#     RemoteSettingsClient.get():476-500 has a `syncIfEmpty` path: when a
#     collection has no local data AND no JSON dump (`_importJSONDump()` at
#     :483 returns < 0), it calls `RemoteSettings.pullStartupBundle()` at :493
#     -- the ONLY caller of it in the whole tree -- and
#     that downloads ONE attachment, `<attachments>/bundles/startup.json.mozlz4`
#     (Utils.fetchChangesetsBundle():575-600), containing the changesets of
#     EVERY collection, and imports all of them into the local database.
#     Android has plenty of legitimate consumers of dumpless collections
#     (url-classifier-exceptions, tracking-protection-lists, query-stripping,
#     remote-permissions, fingerprinting-protection-overrides ...), so this
#     fires on any fresh profile.
#
#     MEASURED on the LW-M4-13 APK, wiped profile, plain startup, 150 s idle,
#     nothing asked for nimbus and no nimbus module was touched by the probe:
#     the profile's remote-settings IndexedDB then holds 18 collections,
#     including main/nimbus-desktop-experiments with 241 records
#     (lastModified 1787064911213), main/nimbus-secure-experiments with 16, and
#     main/normandy-recipes-capabilities, main/cfr and main/message-groups --
#     normandy and the messaging system not even being built for Android. The
#     app's uid moved rx+12,589,592 tx+256,656 bytes in that window and the
#     capture shows firefox-settings-attachments.cdn.mozilla.net.
#     password-recipes, which DOES ship a dump, has lastModified null: dumps are
#     not imported unless a client asks, so the 18 came over the wire.
#
#     So the honest statement is: the nimbus RECIPES reach the device, inside a
#     bundle that is not nimbus's and that nimbus did not request. What does not
#     happen is any per-client nimbus request (no nimbus-* .sql in the Kotlin
#     RemoteSettingsService's app_remote-settings/, no rs-experiment-loader
#     timer, no experimenter host in any capture), and -- with this patch --
#     nothing can ever read those records: ExperimentManager.onStartup() is
#     never reached, so no enrolment can happen from the local copy either.
#     Closing the bundle itself is LW-M4-08's (services.settings.server), and it
#     cannot be done by dropping the fetch: the same bundle carries the URL
#     classifier and tracking-protection lists this browser needs.
#
#  3. TWO OF ITS resource:// DEPENDENCIES ARE NOT PACKAGED ON ANDROID.
#     ExperimentAPI.sys.mjs:16 lazily imports
#     resource://normandy/lib/CleanupManager.sys.mjs and :28-32 lazily imports
#     resource://messaging-system/lib/Logger.sys.mjs for `lazy.log`. Neither
#     `resource normandy` nor `resource messaging-system` appears in the APK
#     omni.ja's chrome.manifest -- the only resource lines are pdf.js, autofill,
#     content-accessible, gre-resources, nimbus, passwordmgr and three devtools
#     ones. So on Android the FIRST `lazy.log.*` call in this component throws,
#     and so does init()'s CleanupManager.addCleanupHandler() at :356.
#     RemoteSettingsExperimentLoader.sys.mjs:25,32 and
#     lib/{ExperimentManager,Enrollments,Migrations}.sys.mjs are in the same
#     position. A half-working component whose error paths throw is not a
#     privacy guarantee; it is a component whose behaviour nobody can predict.
#
#  4. `enabled` THROWS -- and if it ever stops throwing it flips to TRUE.
#     RemoteSettingsExperimentLoader.enable():285 is `if
#     (!lazy.ExperimentAPI.enabled) return;`. `enabled` is
#     `labsEnabled || rolloutsEnabled || studiesEnabled`, and labsEnabled --
#     evaluated first -- is a bare `Services.policies.isAllowed("FirefoxLabs")`.
#     Services.policies is UNDEFINED on Android
#     (toolkit/components/enterprisepolicies/moz.build:16 keeps components.conf,
#     which declares js_name 'policies', inside an `!= "android"` branch), so
#     that is a TypeError, swallowed by init()'s try/catch at :330-333.
#
#     THIS IS THE ACTUAL HOLE. labsEnabled reads no pref. Both LibreWolf
#     lockPrefs -- app.shield.optoutstudies.enabled and nimbus.rollouts.enabled
#     -- are irrelevant to it. If anything ever defines Services.policies on
#     Android, or upstream writes `Services.policies?.isAllowed(...)` itself,
#     labsEnabled becomes true (nothing disallows FirefoxLabs: our
#     settings/distribution/policies.json has no such key), `enabled` becomes
#     true, and the loader enables and syncs
#     nimbus-desktop-experiments from firefox.settings.services.mozilla.com --
#     with our two lockPrefs still in place and still looking like they cover
#     this. That is exactly the shape of defect this project keeps finding.
#
