# WHAT THIS PATCH DOES, AND WHAT IT DELIBERATELY DOES NOT DO
# ---------------------------------------------------------
# Three hunks, one file, no new file in the tree, nothing generated.
#
#   1. init() returns false immediately on Android. This is the one entry point
#      that turns the component on. Not a throw and not a silent success:
#      `false` is init()'s own documented "was not initialized" value, and its
#      only Android-reachable caller (BackgroundTasksUtils.enableNimbus)
#      ignores the return value. #initializedPromise stays null, the four pref
#      observers are never registered, the enrollment store is never opened.
#      No log call here, for the reason in accident 3.
#
#   2. #computeEnabled() returns before reading anything, leaving the cached
#      values at their initialisers (rollouts/studies/telemetry false,
#      aiFeatures "blocked"), so studiesEnabled and rolloutsEnabled cannot
#      become true whatever the prefs say. That matters for
#      RemoteSettingsExperimentLoader.onEnabledPrefChange(), finishedUpdating()
#      and _optInToExperiment() -- the last being the one path that fetches a
#      recipe without enable() ever running, gated on studiesEnabled at
#      lib/RemoteSettingsExperimentLoader.sys.mjs:769.
#
#      BUT BE HONEST ABOUT WHAT THIS HUNK IS: with hunk 1 in place it is
#      DEFENCE IN DEPTH, not an independently observable behaviour, and an
#      earlier version of this header overstated it. #computeEnabled() has
#      exactly three call sites -- twice inside #init() and once inside
#      _onEnabledPrefChange(), which itself returns immediately when
#      #initializedPromise is null. Hunk 1 stops #init() ever running, so
#      #initializedPromise is null forever and #computeEnabled() is unreachable
#      on Android anyway. LW-M4-15 tried to separate the two on the running
#      build and COULD NOT: after removing accidents 3 and 4, unlocking and
#      setting app.shield.optoutstudies.enabled, datareporting.healthreport.
#      uploadEnabled and nimbus.rollouts.enabled to true and browser.ai.control.
#      default to "available", and calling _onEnabledPrefChange(), the patched
#      build left studiesEnabled/rolloutsEnabled/aiFeaturesEnabled all false --
#      an outcome hunk 1 alone predicts just as well. The stock build, same
#      sequence, flipped all three to true. Keep the hunk (it is free, and it
#      makes the guarantee independent of hunk 1 surviving a rebase), but do not
#      cite it as separately measured.
#
#   3. labsEnabled becomes `Services.policies?.isAllowed("FirefoxLabs") ?? false`.
#      This is the hole from accident 4, and it is the only hunk that is not
#      merely a short-circuit: labsEnabled reads no pref, so nothing else in
#      this patch or in librewolf.cfg can make it false.
#
# NOT DONE, on purpose:
#
#   * `DIRS += ["nimbus"]` is NOT guarded out. It cannot be: lib/NimbusFeatures.cpp
#     is compiled into libxul, and `mozilla/browser/NimbusFeatures.h` -- exported
#     by that same moz.build line 8,
#     `EXPORTS.mozilla.browser += ["!lib/NimbusFeatureManifest.h", ...]` -- is
#     #included from C++ that is NOT under browser/ and does ship on Android:
#     netwerk/protocol/http/HttpBaseChannel.cpp:36 (call at :3488,
#     NimbusFeatures::RecordExposureEvent("opaqueResponseBlocking")) and
#     toolkit/components/antitracking/bouncetrackingprotection/
#     BounceTrackingProtection.cpp:48 (call at :1019). The only other includer
#     is browser/components/about/AboutRedirector.cpp:17, which is desktop-only.
#     (An earlier draft of this header named KeySystemConfig.cpp, GMPParent.cpp,
#     UtilityProcessHost.cpp and Preferences.cpp here. That was wrong -- none of
#     those four mentions NimbusFeatures at all; verified by
#     `grep -rIl NimbusFeatures --include=*.cpp --include=*.h`, whose whole
#     non-test answer is the three files above plus nimbus's own two.)
#     Dropping the directory therefore breaks the build, and
#     dropping only the jar.mn would break every consumer that imports
#     resource://nimbus/ExperimentAPI.sys.mjs. There are 24 such modules outside
#     browser/, mobile/, tests, and nimbus's and normandy's own; 19 of them are
#     in this APK's omni.ja -- ConfigSearchEngine, LoginManagerParent,
#     FormAutofillParent, DoHController, PictureInPictureChild,
#     ContentRelevancyManager, IPPNimbusHelper, FirstStartup, Troubleshoot, the
#     three DAP modules, four telemetry modules and the two backgroundtasks ones
#     -- and all of them reach NimbusFeatures.*.getVariable() (the JS export of
#     the same name, not the C++ class above).
#     Those calls are safe: getVariable() reads the
#     ExperimentStore, which with no enrollments returns the FeatureManifest
#     default, and ExperimentAPI.ready() is documented as NOT initialising the
#     API ("doing so breaks a lot of tests due to enabling the
#     RemoteSettingsExperimentLoader"), so it simply never resolves on Android.
#
#   * The `nimbus.profileId` user pref is NOT removed. The ExperimentAPI
#     constructor mints a UUID into it (ExperimentAPI.sys.mjs:198-215) the first
#     time the module is imported in the parent process -- a persistent
#     per-profile identifier. It DOES happen on Android: measured, the pref has
#     a user value on a running build, because something imports the module at
#     startup (see MEASURED above). This patch does not stop that -- hunk 1 is
#     in init(), not in the constructor -- and the pref is inert with the patch
#     applied (nothing sends it: the RecordedNimbusContext/Glean path is
#     LW-M4-01's). Removing it would mean editing a constructor four other
#     things depend on, and it would not stop the import. Flagged for M5 parity
#     rather than fixed here, now as a measured fact rather than a suspicion.
#
#   * `services.settings.server` is not touched. Blocking remote settings
#     wholesale is LW-M4-08's; this patch removes one collection's client, the
#     way no-nimbus.patch removes the Kotlin SDK's, and does not touch the
#     shared service that search config and the blocklist also use.
#
#   * Nothing in this patch changes fission.webContentIsolationStrategy. The
#     effective value stays 2 on all four channels, from
#     patches/android/no-nimbus.patch's FML default; LW-M5-01 owns the 1-vs-2
#     decision and the strength order is 1 > 2 > 0.
#
#
# WHY AN ANDROID PATCH THAT EDITS A COMMON FILE, AND WHY THE PLATFORM GUARDS
# -------------------------------------------------------------------------
# ExperimentAPI.sys.mjs is a shared toolkit file, not an Android-only one; this
# patch is in assets/patches/android.txt, so it is only ever applied to an
# Android build and the desktop tree stays byte-identical.
# scripts/lint-patch-scope.py permits it because the rule for android.txt is
# "touches no DESKTOP-ONLY file", and this file is demonstrably not desktop-only
# (it is in the Android APK's omni.ja).
#
# The `AppConstants.platform === "android"` guards are therefore redundant
# today, and they are kept deliberately, for one reason: they make the patch
# safe to promote to common.txt without re-deciding anything. If a later task
# concludes that desktop LibreWolf should also stop syncing
# nimbus-desktop-experiments -- which it currently DOES, because labsEnabled is
# true there -- moving this file between lists must not silently change desktop
# behaviour as a side effect. With the guards, moving it is a no-op for desktop
# and the desktop decision stays a separate, deliberate change.
#
#
# Ordering: no constraint. No other patch in common.txt, desktop.txt or
# android.txt touches toolkit/components/nimbus/ -- checked with
#   grep -rn '^+++ b/toolkit/components/nimbus' patches/
# which returns only this file.
#
# Nothing happens outside this diff (landmine L4): no file copy, no rm, no
# generated file edited by hand.
--- a/toolkit/components/nimbus/ExperimentAPI.sys.mjs
+++ b/toolkit/components/nimbus/ExperimentAPI.sys.mjs
@@ -260,6 +260,31 @@
    *          Whether or not the ExperimentAPI was initialized.
    */
   async init({ extraContext, forceSync = false } = {}) {
+    // LibreWolf (Android): the JS Nimbus client is never initialized.
+    //
+    // This is the single entry point that turns the component on: it is what
+    // constructs the RemoteSettingsExperimentLoader (via the #experimentLoader
+    // lazy getter), calls its enable(), which registers the
+    // "rs-experiment-loader-timer" update timer and calls updateRecipes(),
+    // which is the only code in this component that touches the network.
+    // Nothing else in the tree constructs that loader.
+    //
+    // Returning here is fail-closed rather than fail-quiet: init() returning
+    // false is its documented "was not initialized" value, #initializedPromise
+    // stays null, no pref observer is registered, and no enrollment store is
+    // opened, so nothing can re-enable the loader later through
+    // _onEnabledPrefChange.
+    //
+    // Deliberately no log call: lazy.log resolves
+    // resource://messaging-system/lib/Logger.sys.mjs, and messaging-system is
+    // NOT packaged on Android (toolkit/components/moz.build only adds it for
+    // MOZ_BUILD_APP == "browser"; verified absent from the APK's
+    // omni.ja chrome.manifest, which maps "resource nimbus" and no
+    // "resource messaging-system"). Logging here would throw.
+    if (AppConstants.platform === "android") {
+      return false;
+    }
+
     if (this.#initializedPromise) {
       // Either init has already finished, or it is in flight. Either way,
       // chain off the promise so we only return once init is actually complete.
@@ -454,6 +479,19 @@
   }
 
   #computeEnabled() {
+    // LibreWolf (Android): leave every cached value at its initialiser, i.e.
+    // rolloutsEnabled/studiesEnabled/telemetryEnabled false and
+    // aiFeaturesEnabled "blocked". Combined with the labsEnabled hunk below
+    // this makes `enabled` false on Android by construction rather than by
+    // pref value, which closes three call sites the init() hunk does not:
+    // RemoteSettingsExperimentLoader.onEnabledPrefChange(), its
+    // finishedUpdating(), and _optInToExperiment() -- the last of which is the
+    // one path that fetches a recipe without the loader ever being enabled,
+    // and it is gated on studiesEnabled.
+    if (AppConstants.platform === "android") {
+      return;
+    }
+
     this.#prefValues.rolloutsEnabled = Services.prefs.getBoolPref(
       Prefs.ROLLOUTS_ENABLED,
       false
@@ -482,7 +520,22 @@
   }
 
   get labsEnabled() {
-    return Services.policies.isAllowed("FirefoxLabs");
+    // LibreWolf: optional call plus an explicit false, because Services.policies
+    // is UNDEFINED on Android -- toolkit/components/enterprisepolicies/
+    // moz.build:16 puts components.conf (which declares js_name 'policies')
+    // inside an `!= "android"` branch, so the contract ID has no
+    // implementation registered and the js_name property is never defined.
+    //
+    // The bare call therefore threw a TypeError here, and that accident was
+    // load-bearing: `enabled` is `labsEnabled || rolloutsEnabled ||
+    // studiesEnabled`, labsEnabled is evaluated FIRST, and it reads no pref -
+    // so on desktop, where policies exists and no policy disallows
+    // FirefoxLabs, it is true and `enabled` is true no matter what
+    // app.shield.optoutstudies.enabled and nimbus.rollouts.enabled say. The
+    // moment anything defines Services.policies on Android, Nimbus would
+    // become "enabled" again with both LibreWolf lockPrefs still in place.
+    // Relying on an exception for that is not a barrier; this is.
+    return Services.policies?.isAllowed("FirefoxLabs") ?? false;
   }
 
   get rolloutsEnabled() {
